<pdd-reason>Preserve the established SO(3), Euler, Hamilton quaternion and Goldstein Cayley–Klein convention dictionary.</pdd-reason>
<pdd-version>0.1.0</pdd-version>

% Deliverable and ownership
Generate only `rotations.py`, a Python >=3.11 module using only the standard library.
Do not generate or modify rigid_body.py, simulator_server.py, tests, validation
oracles, notes, native libraries, pyproject.toml or this project configuration.
No NumPy/SciPy/SymPy dependency in this module. No I/O or import-time side effects.

% Approved requirements (complete request; do not truncate)
As a researcher who uses the rotation library in Python/rotations to check Goldstein, Frankel and engineering attitude conventions, I want the library's conventions written down as the source it is regenerated from, so that a regenerated version gives the same rotations, angles and quaternions as the current one.

The library MUST keep right-handed column vectors with x_I = R x_B, and Goldstein's coordinate matrix MUST be the transpose A_G = R.T.
Euler angles MUST be given and returned in intrinsic product order: ZXZ and ZYZ as (phi, theta, psi), and ZYX as (heading, pitch, roll) meaning Rz(heading) Ry(pitch) Rx(roll).
Extraction MUST return the middle angle in [0, pi] for ZXZ/ZYZ and in [-pi/2, pi/2] for ZYX; at gimbal lock it MUST set the last angle to zero and report singular=True.
Quaternions MUST be scalar-first Hamilton quaternions, and matrix-to-quaternion extraction MUST return the representative with w >= 0.
Axis-angle extraction MUST return an angle in [0, pi] and MUST use the axis (1, 0, 0) for the identity.
Cayley-Klein parameters MUST satisfy gamma = -conj(beta) and delta = conj(alpha), with alpha = w + i z and beta = y + i x.
The library MUST reject non-finite values, reflections, non-unit quaternions, non-unit axes and CK parameters that break the conjugacy constraints.
The library MUST NOT silently project accepted data onto SO(3); the only normalization allowed is the documented one inside matrix-to-quaternion extraction.
The rotation functions MUST NOT depend on anything outside the Python standard library.
Never return a ZYX tuple in (roll, pitch, yaw) order.

For example, from_euler('zyx', 0.4, 0.7, -0.3) followed by to_euler(R, 'zyx') returns (0.4, 0.7, -0.3) with singular=False.
For example, a 90 degree turn about z maps the body x axis to the inertial y axis, and to_quaternion returns (cos(pi/4), 0, 0, sin(pi/4)).

% Stable public interface
Preserve the existing names, argument order and defaults:
- `Matrix = tuple[tuple[float, ...], ...]`
- `I3 = ((1., 0., 0.), (0., 1., 0.), (0., 0., 1.))`
- `transpose(a)`
- `multiply(a, b)`
- `apply(a, x)`
- `det(a)`
- `validate_matrix(a, tolerance=1e-10)`
- `hat(x)`
- `elementary(axis, phi)`
- `from_euler(convention, first, middle, last)`
- Frozen dataclass `EulerAngles(first: float, middle: float, last: float, singular: bool)`, with read-only `values` property returning `(first, middle, last)`.
- `to_euler(a, convention, singular_tolerance=1e-12)`
- `validate_quaternion(q, tolerance=1e-10)`
- `quaternion_product(q, p)`
- `from_quaternion(q)`
- `to_quaternion(a)`
- `from_axis_angle(axis, phi)`
- `to_axis_angle(a)`
- `quaternion_to_ck(q)`
- `ck_to_quaternion(parameters, tolerance=1e-10)`
- `ck_to_goldstein(parameters)`
- `cayley_hamilton_residual(a)`

% Representation and validation
Matrices are nested real sequences; return immutable tuples of tuples, and
return tuples for vectors, quaternions and CK parameters. Never mutate inputs.
transpose, multiply and apply accept nonempty rectangular matrices; reject
ragged/empty matrices, incompatible dimensions and nonfinite components with
ValueError. det requires a 3x3 matrix. hat requires a finite three-vector and
returns the cross-product matrix, so hat(x)y = x cross y.

validate_matrix requires 3x3 finite real values, R.T R = I and det(R) = +1
within the supplied tolerance. Return the supplied components converted to
float tuples, without re-orthogonalizing or projecting them. Tolerances must
be finite and in (0, 1e-4]. Default matrix/quaternion/CK tolerance is 1e-10;
the unit-axis check uses |norm(axis)-1| <= 1e-10. Quaternion validation uses
|sum(q_i^2)-1| <= tolerance. Conversion outputs are validated too, so an input
within one check's tolerance can still fail a tighter downstream check.
All invalid domain values raise ValueError. Do not silently normalize imported
quaternions, axes, matrices or CK parameters. to_quaternion first validates R,
then normalizes the extracted quaternion to remove floating-point roundoff.

% Constructions and branch behavior
Use standard right-handed elementary matrices Rx, Ry, Rz. Axis names and
conventions are lowercase and restricted to x/y/z and zxz/zyz/zyx respectively.
Euler construction accepts any finite angles, composing exactly in product
order. Extraction returns first/last via atan2 (principal representatives),
with canonical middle ranges specified above. At/within singular_tolerance
(default 1e-12), choose last=0 and singular=True; reconstruct within O(tolerance).
At lock for ZXZ, first=atan2(R[1][0],R[0][0]); for ZYZ use the same
unless R[2][2]<0, when first=atan2(-R[1][0],-R[0][0]); for ZYX use
first=atan2(-R[0][1],R[1][1]). Away from lock singular=False. Cover both poles for each convention.
Quaternion product is Hamilton q*p, corresponding to R(q)R(p); it validates
both operands and the result. q and -q map to the same rotation.
Use stable largest-diagonal quaternion extraction at/near pi, canonical w>=0.
Rodrigues construction should use 2*sin(phi/2)^2 to avoid cancellation near zero.
Axis-angle extraction through the quaternion must remain stable near pi.
quaternion_to_ck returns (alpha,beta,-conj(beta),conj(alpha)); inverse extraction
returns (Re(alpha),Im(beta),Re(beta),Im(alpha)) after checking conjugacy and norm.
ck_to_goldstein returns transpose(from_quaternion(ck_to_quaternion(parameters))).
cayley_hamilton_residual validates R then returns the actual polynomial matrix
R^3 - tr(R)R^2 + tr(R)R - I, rather than an assumed zero matrix.

% Independent acceptance
Existing tests/test_rotations.py and tests/test_rotation_conventions.py are
independent acceptance sources, not generated outputs. Preserve their ability
to detect swapped Euler order, quaternion sign, CK conjugacy, reflection,
nonfinite data, silent normalization, singular metadata and third-party imports.
Run from this scope: python -m unittest discover -s tests -p 'test_rotation*.py'.
Run the containing repository's full Python suite using its existing uv venv
as documented in ../../PDD.md. Do not rewrite tests to make generation pass.
